\section{Stress-Test Hamiltonians and Perturbation Models}

Let $H_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}$ denote the unified engine Hamiltonian. Stress-testing
requires introducing perturbation Hamiltonians:
\[
H_{\epsilon} = H_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}} + \epsilon\,\Delta H,
\]
where $\Delta H$ models:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Magnetic quench events.
  \item Thermal overload spikes.
  \item Relativistic exhaust fluctuations.
  \item Fusion pulse asymmetries.
  \item Antimatter extraction jitter.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[TSSE Stress Hamiltonian]
A stress Hamiltonian $H_{\epsilon}$ satisfies:
\[
\lim_{\epsilon \to 0} H_{\epsilon} = H_{\text{TSSE–AITFE}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Perturbation Admissibility]
If $\Delta H$ preserves TSSE symplecticity, then $H_{\epsilon}$ is TSSE-admissible
for sufficiently small $\epsilon$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Symplecticity is preserved under small Hamiltonian perturbations. Therefore,
$H_{\epsilon}$ remains admissible for $\epsilon$ sufficiently small.
\end{proof}

This lemma establishes the foundation for TSSE stress-testing.

