\section{Temporal Symmetry Stress Metrics and Reversal Audits}

Define the temporal symmetry deviation metric:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t) =
\left\|
\mathcal{T}\mathcal{U}_{\epsilon}(t) - \mathcal{U}_{\epsilon}(t)\mathcal{T}
\right\|.
\]

\begin{definition}[Temporal Symmetry Stress Metric]
The temporal symmetry stress metric $\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t)$ measures deviation
from perfect temporal symmetry under stress Hamiltonian evolution.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Temporal Symmetry Stability]
If $\Delta H$ commutes with $\mathcal{T}$, then:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t) = 0.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $[\Delta H,\mathcal{T}] = 0$, then $[\mathcal{T},H_{\epsilon}] = 0$, ensuring
temporal symmetry preservation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Temporal Symmetry Robustness]
If $\Delta H$ is TSSE-admissible, then:
\[
\delta_{\mathcal{T}}(t) = \mathcal{O}(\epsilon).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Temporal symmetry deviation scales linearly with perturbation magnitude for
TSSE-admissible perturbations.
\end{proof}

This theorem establishes the robustness of TSSE temporal symmetry under stress.

